On Oct 13, 7:17 am, TJR <tilmina...@googlemail.com> wrote:
> > Nazarite:
>
> In short, no corpses, no booze or juice, and no t-shirts. Effectively,
> this conduct only means you must cast polymorph on all your corpes
> before you eat them, and can't retrieve death drops.

Actually, I think you'd be able to retrieve death drops in cases where
there's no corpse, or after the corpse rots away or something else
eats it.

Casting polymorph on corpses, if you were playing as (say) a wizard,
would make this conduct easier to achieve.  If you want to increase
the challenge level, play as a Valkyrie or Samuria and attempt the
Nazarite conduct -- or of course you can start combining it with other
conducts.

Nazarite was intended to add flavor to the conduct system (that is, a
different type of restriction than existing conducts), more than
difficulty.  Several existing conducts are already harder, as far as
that goes.  If you're looking for something difficult, I'd suggest the
Ascetic conduct.  (If you're looking for something borderline
impossible, try for magic-free.)

> Most nethack nudist descriptions still allow rings, amulets, and
> eyewear. In my opinion, it would be a good idea to point to a nudist
> ascension (allowing P stuff) before ruling out even that.

Ah.  In that case, call this one Strict Nudist or Total Nudist or
something.

And yeah, I'm not entirely sure what the difficulty would be for this
one.  My gut instinct suggests it's probably not as hard as ascetic,
but that's really just a guess.  I haven't experimented with it
substantially.

> > Magic-Free:
>
> The game already classifies objects as magic for polypiling purposes,
> why not just re-use that attribute, since you're close anyway?

The intent is that you must not use magic, not that you must not use
magical objects.  You'd be allowed to use an object that has magical
properties, if you're not using it for its magical properties.
Granted, I can't think of a lot of good examples where this
distinction matters very much, other than dipping magical potions into
water to make potions of water.

Note too that I didn't classify religion (prayer, in particular, but
also altars and priests and whatnot) as magical.  There's already an
atheist conduct, so if you want to prohibit both, just combine magic-
free with atheist -- although, as I said, magic-free is extremely
difficult as it stands and shouldn't need to be combined with other
conducts.


